d those. So discarding B2 or T2 might be relatively safe.\n\nGiven I have a pair of T2, discarding one T2 could be reasonable to avoid having too many pairs (currently 5 pairs, which is too many for a standard hand; I need to convert some pairs into melds). Discarding a T2 reduces to 4 pairs, which is still high but better. Also, T2 is a simple tile, not terminal, so it doesn't hurt All Simples.\n\nAlternatively, discarding B2 reduces a singleton and keeps all pairs, but that leaves me with 5 pairs, which might stall development because I need to break pairs to form chows.\n\nI think discarding one of the pair tiles to reduce pair count is good. Among my pairs, which is least valuable? T9 is terminal, which could give points if I pung it, but terminals are harder to complete since many are dead (W9 dead, T1/T9 less dead). T2 and T3 are simple, abundant. B3 is simple. W8 is simple. T9 might be the most risky to keep because it's terminal and less flexible for chows (only chow with T7-T8-T9). But I have T8 and T9 pair, so keeping T9 might lead to a chow if I draw T7. However, discarding one T9 breaks the pair and frees up tiles.\n\nI'll evaluate tile counts: T9 unseen: 3 left. So pung chance is moderate. But since I have T8, a chow is also possible. I think keeping the T9 pair is okay.\n\nPerhaps discard T2: it's a simple tile, I have two, and breaking one pair might be beneficial. Also, T2 can be used in chows with T1/T3, but I have T3 pair, so T1-T2-T3 is possible if I draw T1 (2 left). However, previous player exposed T1-T2-T3, so T1 might be in demand? But there are 2 left. It's uncertain.\n\nConsidering the game stage (many tiles played), I should aim for efficiency. I'll simulate: if I discard T2, my hand becomes: W2, W6, W8x2, T2, T3x2, T8, T9x2, B2, B3x2. That's 4 pairs (W8, T3, T9, B3) and some singletons. I can then aim to draw connecting tiles to form chows.\n\nAnother option: discard T3 similarly.\n\nI think discarding a tile from a pair of simple tiles is reasonable. T2 or T3 are good candidates.\n\nI'll choose T2 because it was discarded by next player earlier, indicating it might be safe.\n\nReason: My hand is fragmented with multiple pairs and singletons. To progress towards a standard hand, I need to reduce pair count and develop sequences. Discarding one T2 maintains a pair of T3 and keeps other pairs, while T2 is a safe discard given its history and it's a simple tile, preserving All Simples potential. It also keeps flexibility with T3 for possible chows.\n\nFinal choice: Play T2.\n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
